# PROPOSED — NOT IMPORTED BY deploy/caddy/Caddyfile (website-10, 2026-10-12; the owner's review item 1). The owner applies host changes.
#
# What it does: the apex `/` is answered by the Next app's home (/burnticker: the one-line hero, How it works, the coin grid, Follow the
# season) instead of the static landing page. Every other static path (/whitepaper.html, /brand/*, /status/, /tokens.html, /t/<ticker>/…,
# /.well-known/…) keeps coming from /var/www/burnticker exactly as today.
#
# Why it is safe to apply later and not needed now: since website-10 the static site/burnticker/index.html is already a thin page in the
# app's shell (same header, footer receipt, tab bar and activity line, generated from src/lib/burnticker/siteNav.mjs) that client-renders
# the featured cards from GET /api/burnticker/home. Swapping `/` to the app only changes who renders the same page. When the app is down
# or slow, `/` falls back to that static page, so the root never shows an error.
#
# How to apply (on the Burnticker VPS, after review):
#   1. paste the `handle @home` block below into the {$BURNTICKER_DOMAIN:burnticker.com} site block of /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, ABOVE the
#      `@app path /burnticker …` block (handle blocks with named matchers run in the order they appear);
#   2. `caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile`, then `systemctl reload caddy`;
#   3. check: `curl -sI https://burnticker.com/` answers 200 from the app (an `x-nextjs-*` header is present), and
#      `curl -s https://burnticker.com/whitepaper.html | head -1` is still the static file.
# To undo: delete the block and reload. Nothing else on the host changes.
#
# Notes for the reviewer:
#   - The matcher is the exact path `/` (and `/index.html`, which some crawlers and old links request). It does NOT widen the @app matcher
#     to `/` or `/*`; the security note at the top of the live Caddyfile still holds.
#   - The public Host header passes through unchanged (no header_up Host), as in the live @app block.
#   - The canonical URL of the home stays https://burnticker.com/ (the app's /burnticker page declares /burnticker as canonical today; when
#     this proposal is applied, set `alternates.canonical` in src/app/burnticker/page.tsx to "/" in the same release).
#   - Cache: the same Cache-Control rewrite as the @app pages (a CDN may keep the shell 60 s, then revalidate).

@home path / /index.html
handle @home {
	import security
	rewrite * /burnticker
	reverse_proxy {$MCT_APP_UPSTREAM:127.0.0.1:3000} {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto {scheme}
		header_down Cache-Control "s-maxage=31536000" "public, max-age=0, s-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300"
		transport http {
			dial_timeout 3s
			response_header_timeout 20s
		}
		# the app answering 5xx: serve the static home (the same shell, rendered client-side from the API) instead of an error page
		@upstream_5xx status 5xx
		handle_response @upstream_5xx {
			root * /var/www/burnticker
			rewrite * /index.html
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			file_server
		}
	}
}

# And inside the existing `handle_errors` block, so an unreachable app (Caddy's own 502/504) also gets the static home at `/`:
